  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 AM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nToday through Sat night...The closed low that has ejected\nshortwaves into the Pacific Northwest for the past three days\nwill itself have moved onshore by this afternoon. As it gets\ncloser, southerly wind and abundant moisture will push into\nIdaho from the south, so best chances of precpitation will be in\nthe southern portions of the forecast area. The southerly flow\ndoes keep afternoon temperatures about the same or warmer than\nWednesday, so morning snow and some higher elevation snow will\nchange over to rainfall, while the snow stays consistent above\n6000ft elevation in the south and 5500ft elevation in the north.\nThe highest snowfall accumulations will be in the Wood River\nValley basin and Big Lost River highlands. The Winter Weather\nAdvisory continues in effect in this location, as the snow has\nalready started and solid snowfall is likely during the day.\nThere will be a slackening of precipitation accumulation for any\nlocation for about 12 hours, then another surge of moisture\nmoves through on Fri. This time the focus of precipitation will\nbe eastern areas, especially the southeast highlands. Strong\ntapering off occurs everywhere Fri night with Sat being light\nprecipitation at best. Sat night is also fairly dry across the\nforecast area; this makes it the best day for travel this\nThanksgiving holiday. \n\nThe southerly flow that keeps temperatures mild today shifts to\na colder but weaker northwesterly flow, so temperatures will\ncool on Fri, with very few locations reaching 40 degrees. On\nSat, the clearing skies allow a larger area to reach the low\n40s. Cloud cover keeps lows in the teens to low 30s tonight;\nreduced cloud cover, especially in the central Idaho mountains,\nwill allow temperatures to cool.\n\nWind this morning will be strong but below Wind Advisory\ncriteria in the southern hills and the southern side of the \nMagic Valley. However, they will be decreasing and not expected\nto be as windy for the rest of the short term. Expect breezy\ncondtions again Fri afternoon, with light wind on Sat.\n\nSun through Wed...After the brief upper level ridge on Sat, a\nfaster moving open wave approaches the west coast. There may not\nbe much precipitation that day, which makes it also a good\ntravel day, unless you are headed west into OR or WA. For Mon,\nmost clusters have a low amplitude trough zipping through\nsouthern Idaho. Temperatures will struggle to get above freezing\non Mon, which would mean more snow, although the weakness of\nthis trough means likely not very much. For Tue, all but 10\npercent of the clusters have an upper level ridge just to the\nwest that favors fair weather, but no warm up in temperature.\nThis ridge may or not break down on Wed. A way-out forecast for\nThanksgiving Day indicates upper level ridging the current most\nlikely expectation, which could allow for a sunny day and quiet\nconditions for the holiday itself. Wed trough appears to be\naffecting the front range of the Colorado Rockies by that time.",
